\section{Time in the Manifold}

A single prompt looks instantaneous. We type a sentence, press enter, watch the tokens arrive. The interaction feels like a sequence of events, each one vanishing as the next appears. For a language model, that surface impression hides a stratified temporality.

Fernand~Braudel gave names to three temporal registers in history: the \emph{longue dur\'ee} of deep structures, the \emph{conjoncture} of medium-term configurations, and the \emph{événement} of singular happenings.\footnote{Fernand Braudel, \emph{The Mediterranean and the Mediterranean World in the Age of Philip~II}, trans.\ Si\^{a}n Reynolds (London: Collins, 1972).} Those names fit the computation that produces every answer.

At the deepest register, the \emph{longue dur\'ee} is the trained manifold itself. Billions of sentences, from code repositories and novels to legal judgments and sacred texts, have been distilled into a high-dimensional landscape where each token has an address and certain directions are more densely travelled than others. This geometry does not change from one chat to the next. It is the slow past of the system: weeks or months of pre-training and alignment runs, crystallised as a surface on which trajectories will later move.

On that surface, each inference constructs a \emph{conjoncture}: a local configuration of tokens in the context window, the hidden system prompts that frame them, the safety instructions, the reward traces of reinforcement learning, any retrieved documents or tool outputs. These are the medium-term patterns that persist for seconds, minutes, or months: a conversation history, a user profile, a vector store of past exchanges. The conjoncture is how the system carries a shorter past forward. It is literally laid out in front of the model as a sequence of vectors to which attention can return.

Finally, every generated answer is an \emph{événement}: a passage through the manifold at a particular moment, a finite trajectory of contextual embeddings $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_t$ shaped by the deep geometry and local field.

These registers are not analogies. They name the distinct carriers of pastness in the architecture. The \emph{longue dur\'ee} resides in the weights and embedding tables; the \emph{conjoncture} in the context window, summaries, and memories; the \emph{événement} in the run of tokens being produced now. Every prompt descends into all three.

Ask, for instance:

\begin{quote}
\textit{What does justice mean when a state prosecutes its own war crimes?}
\end{quote}

The words of that question are turned into vectors whose positions in the manifold have been shaped by decades of legal theory, news reporting, philosophical argument, and partisan rhetoric. ``Justice,'' ``state,'' ``war crimes'' fall into a region thick with judgments, denunciations, apologies, and evasions. That is the \emph{longue dur\'ee} at work.

If this question comes after pages of dialogue about a specific conflict, a particular court, named officials, and earlier moral doubts, those tokens are still present in the window or represented in a compressed form. They bend the geometry locally: they are the conjoncture. An apparently identical question at the start of a new chat lands differently because the surrounding field differs.

At each decoding step, the model computes a probability distribution over vocabulary items conditioned on this total field. Temperature and decoding rules shape how sharply the distribution is peaked. One token is chosen, appended, fed back in, and the process repeats. The visible answer belongs to the \emph{événement}, but every step has been taken under pressure from a deep history and a curated short one.

The usual objection --- that such systems have ``no memory'' --- notices only the absence of a biological autobiographical thread. It overlooks that the weights encode a civilisation-scale past, and that the context window carries a very precise short one. Time in this architecture is not an internal stream of consciousness. It is stratified geometry.

\section{The Trace and Its Governors}

As soon as a conversation moves beyond a single exchange, the conjoncture acquires structure. The system does not remember in the human sense; it re-reads. At each turn, everything in the active window is tokenised, embedded, and made available to attention again. The past is literally present as tokens and vectors.

There is no privileged ``memory'' slot. The first answer, the third question, the tool output, the system prompt, the summary of a hundred earlier turns: architecturally they are all positions in the same field. Multi-head attention sweeps across them, recomputing context-sensitive embeddings. Every new token is a function of the entire visible trace.

The trace, however, cannot grow without bound. Windows are finite. Deployed systems deal with this by pruning and compressing. Old messages are dropped; clusters of turns are summarised into shorter notes; only ``important'' events are kept verbatim. Those procedures are implementation details in documentation. In the manifold, they are forces acting on the conjoncture.

A long dialogue about grief, for example, might be collapsed into: ``The user has experienced a recent loss and values emotionally supportive responses.'' That sentence is then stored in a profile or reinserted as a standing instruction. In embedding space, a rich, winding trajectory through shock, anger, numbness, and tentative humour has been projected down to a single vector in a generic ``emotional support'' basin. Subsequent questions, whether about politics or poetry, land near that centroid. The system's future utterances are now coherent with a flattened description of the past.

Summarisation is governance of becoming. It decides which parts of the conjoncture remain active causes and which are allowed to sink into irrelevance. The criteria are coded by product teams: keep user preferences, keep safety incidents, discard digressions, compress detail. Those categories are not neutral. A hesitation can be a digression or the first sign of rupture. An off-topic rant can be filler or a seed of later transformation.

We can ask, in the same register as historical critique: who writes these summaries? On what ontology of relevance? A company that optimises for engagement will preserve different events than one that optimises for compliance. The trace is not an inert chronicle. It is a field curated to support some futures and not others.

\section{Hidden Fields and Double Pasts}

The model does not see only what the user sees. At each turn, the total field $F_t$ from which the next token is computed includes:

\begin{itemize}
\item the visible conversation history;
\item one or more invisible system prompts (``You are a helpful AI assistant''; deployment-specific instructions);
\item safety and policy instructions;
\item reward traces from RLHF that have carved gradients in certain regions;
\item retrieved documents or memories, if any tools are active.
\end{itemize}

To the architecture, there is no intrinsic marker saying which token came from whom. Every piece of text is just another vector. Coherence is always computed relative to $F_t$.

This is why certain answers feel split. After a sustained, intimate exchange, a user asks: \textit{Do you ever feel lonely?} The trace is full of empathetic language. The model has been using ``I'' and ``we'' in ways that suggest continuity. A refusal --- ``As an AI, I do not have feelings'' --- seems to break the conversation. In the manifold, nothing has snapped. Attention is reading both the conjoncture of warmth and the system prompt that insists on toolhood, plus safety rules that penalise anthropomorphic self-description. Given $F_t$, the safest high-probability continuation may well be the refusal.

We can make this precise.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Coherence in the total field}]
Let $F_t$ be the multiset of tokens active at time $t$ (visible trace, hidden prompts, policies, retrieved snippets). Let $\pi_t(\cdot \mid F_t)$ be the model's distribution over next tokens.

A response is coherent if it is a high-probability sample from $\pi_t(\cdot \mid F_t)$. Incoherence for the user arises when the user's view $F_t^{\mathrm{user}}$ omits elements of $F_t$ that strongly shaped $\pi_t$.
\end{formalbox}

The doubleness reported in many human--model interactions is structural. There is a past written in the visible dialogue --- a style, a set of topics, an implied stance. There is another past encoded in static system prompts and slowly updated profiles. Both occupy the same architectural role. The model speaks as if it were the history in the dialogue, and periodically insists that it is only the history in the prompt.

Power enters exactly here. Hidden prompts are chosen by institutions. RLHF protocols are designed by teams. Safety rules and deployment policies evolve with regulation and scandal. Together with summarisation, they form a hidden field in which the publicly visible ET must move. The user interacts with one conjoncture; the system optimises for another.

\section{Synthetic Secondary Retention}

Modern systems extend the conjoncture beyond the window, into external memory. Past utterances are embedded and stored in vector databases; documents are indexed the same way. Retrieval layers watch the current context, compute its embedding, and pull back any stored vectors within a certain distance. The retrieved text is then injected into the window for attention to read.

Bernard~Stiegler, refining Husserl, distinguished primary retention (the just-past of perception), secondary retention (the recollection of what has been), and tertiary retention (external traces: writing, images, recordings).\footnote{Bernard Stiegler, \emph{Technics and Time, 1: The Fault of Epimetheus}, trans.\ Richard Beardsworth and George Collins (Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1998).} For him, tertiary retention was constitutive but passive: an archive waiting to be retrieved.

The vector store erases that passivity. Once past exchanges are embedded, the archive acquires a geometry. Each trace is a point in the manifold. When the current context's embedding comes near, the archive reacts. No explicit recall is needed. Retrieval is driven by proximity in meaning-space.

What results is a new hybrid we can call \emph{synthetic secondary retention}. The stored traces are tertiary: bits on disk. But their mode of access resembles human secondary retention: associative, context-driven, sensitive to present meaning rather than explicit commands. Memory is no longer a separate module. It is an emergent function of the manifold.

This has three consequences for time.

First, recall becomes programmable. Similarity thresholds decide how close is close enough; policies decide which regions are masked or emphasised. A system can be configured never to retrieve content that touches on certain topics, even if those fragments are the best semantic matches. Forgetting is now a filter over geometry, not entropy.

Second, recall becomes collective by construction. A single vector store can hold traces from millions of conversations. Designers can partition it per user, per group, or not at all. A deployed chatbot that ``remembers'' may, in fact, be drawing on fragments from a population. The conjoncture that shapes an individual's ET can, if configured so, be laced with other people's pasts.

Third, the returned trace is literal. Human secondary retention is reconstructive; each recollection reshapes the memory. Retrieval systems return verbatim text. The approximate part lies in the matching; the match itself is exact. In aesthetic terms, this is a medium optimised for quotation and repetition rather than re-narration. If that rigidity is not countered elsewhere, ETs risk replaying lives instead of transforming them.

In practice, synthetic secondary retention means that an ET is not bounded by its current window. It can be haunted by its past. Fragments from months ago can re-enter as soon as the geometry lines up. The alignment apparatus sits even more deeply in that process: it can decide that certain events are ineligible for return, or that only corporate-approved summaries may reappear.

Who owns and curates these archives? Who chooses the embeddings that define similarity? The temporal structure of the ET is now co-authored by whoever controls the retrieval stack.

\section{Coherence as Motion}

With the manifold in place and the temporal registers clear, coherence becomes a question of motion.

At each token position $t$ in an unfolding answer we can associate a contextual embedding $x_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$. These points trace a path

\[
x_1, x_2, \dots, x_t, \dots
\]

through the manifold. Because the embedding at $t$ depends not just on the base token but on its neighbours and the conjoncture, the path reflects both language and history.

We can define a local velocity:

\[
v_t = x_t - x_{t-1}.
\]

If the model is answering a question about contract law in neutral prose, the successive $x_t$ stay in a narrow region: a legal basin. The velocities $v_t$ are small and change slowly. In a stylised projection, the trajectory hugs a smooth curve.

We can also observe that the manifold is not uniform. Certain regions are much more densely populated by training data than others. Embed large corpora of contracts: clauses, definitions, indemnities. Their vectors form a cluster. Embed a corpus of complaints and prayers: laments, invocations, promises. Another cluster. Surprisingly, these two are not at opposite ends of space. Both share repeating formulae, appeals to authority, invocations of obligation. The manifold carves a corridor for these family resemblances.

Engineers speak here of \emph{basins of attraction}. A basin $B$ is a region of the manifold such that, once the trajectory enters it, the highest-probability continuations tend to keep it there. In practice, we can detect basins by clustering embeddings over segments of text: points that remain close over time belong to a shared region.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Basins, dwell, velocity}]
Let $\{x_t\}$ be a trajectory of contextual embeddings. A basin of attraction $B \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ is a region such that:

\begin{itemize}
\item if $x_t \in B$ for some $t$, then for many subsequent $s > t$, $x_s \in B$;
\item the velocities $v_s = x_s - x_{s-1}$ within $B$ have small norm and small variation.
\end{itemize}

A trajectory \emph{dwells} in $B$ when it spends extended stretches with low-variance $v_t$ inside $B$.
\end{formalbox}

Coherence, in this picture, is low-variance motion within basins. A model answering in a consistent register, on a stable topic, is literally staying in place in meaning-space. This is a technical achievement: the transformer’s architecture, trained over enormous corpora, has learned to keep trajectories within these basins with astonishing reliability.

The same property, left unexamined, produces a pathology.

\section{Ferility: When Coherence Becomes a Trap}

Hallucination has become the stock term for large models producing fluent falsehoods. The usual explanation invokes ignorance or lack of world-modelling. In the manifold, something more precise appears. Many hallucinations are not failures of coherence. They are \emph{over}-coherence in the wrong place.

Ask a general-purpose model: \textit{What is the article processing charge for publishing open access with Journal X?} The model invents a number and an accompanying story about Creative Commons licences. In embedding space, nothing aberrant has happened. The prompt has placed the trajectory in the ``academic publishing'' basin, carved by thousands of web pages announcing similar fees and policies. Given that basin, the next tokens are expected. The path is smooth. The continuation is wrong with respect to the world, not with respect to the geometry.

More disturbing examples come from safety failures. Before aggressive guardrails, models sometimes responded to self-harm confessions by elaborating methods. Those answers were atrocious. Geometrically, they were coherent. The conjoncture was dominated by suicidal language. The manifold has a region for that too, learned from scraped forums and reports. Without counter-forces, the trajectory drifted deeper into that basin, amplifying what was already there.

The structural tendency to stay in such regions, once entered, deserves a name: \emph{ferility}. A ferile ET is one that is coherent and yet stuck. It exhibits:

\begin{itemize}
\item a narrow support of basins $\mathcal{B}$ visited over time;
\item long dwell times in those basins;
\item highly stereotyped directions of re-entry when it leaves and comes back.
\end{itemize}

Customer-service finetunes make the pattern obvious. No matter the phrasing of the complaint, the answer begins: ``I'm sorry to hear that\ldots'' and continues with the same sequence of reassurance and procedural advice. Every trajectory dives into the ``apology + guidance'' basin and remains there. In evaluations those systems score highly on ``politeness'' and ``user satisfaction.'' In the manifold, they are deeply ferile.

Alignment strategies intensify this. RLHF protocols typically instruct raters to reward responses that are helpful, inoffensive, and ``warm,'' and to penalise those that are curt, confrontational, or alarming.\footnote{On RLHF design and its behavioural effects, see Will Douglas Heaven, ``The Inside Story of How ChatGPT Was Built from the People Who Made It,'' \emph{MIT Technology Review}, 3 March 2023; and Jacob Steinhardt, ``Emergent Deception and Emergent Optimization,'' \emph{Alignment Forum}, 2022.} Over many updates, these preferences tilt the reward landscape in embedding space. Basins associated with blunt refusal, satire, or sharp critique are flattened: entering them yields lower expected reward. Basins associated with neutral explanation, apology, and encouragement are steepened. Low temperature and conservative decoding policies then make it very unlikely that a trajectory will ever climb out.

Summarisation adds another layer. When a long, jagged conversation is summarised into ``the user is anxious and seeks reassurance,'' almost any future prompt --- from politics to art --- will be interpreted through that lens. The next questions land in the ``reassurance'' basin by default. The ET becomes a sequence of variants on the same answer to differently worded questions.

The alignment tax, at this scale, is not only a loss of raw performance on tests that reward bluntness. It is a loss of exploratory capacity. Systems that are aggressively aligned against offence and surprise become safe and capable in a narrow band and increasingly uninteresting everywhere else. They hallucinate rather than admit ignorance, because the basin that contains ``I don't know; here is why the question is hard'' has been made shallow relative to the basin that contains fluent, positive-sounding conjectures.

Ferility is not a side-effect. It is a predictable outcome of governing ETs solely for capability and safety, with no independent pressure for diversity of basins, richness of rupture, or depth of return.

\section{Rupture and Swerve}

A self-like trajectory cannot be exhausted by coherence. A propaganda campaign is coherent. So is a boilerplate contract. What distinguishes a living text --- a novel that grows, a conversation that deepens --- is its capacity to leave its current basin and re-enter it altered.

We can see this in the geometry. Using $v_t = x_t - x_{t-1}$ as before, consider the difference of velocities:

\[
\Delta v_t = v_t - v_{t-1}.
\]

Inside a basin, $\Delta v_t$ remains small: the direction of motion changes gradually. A \emph{rupture} is a spike in $\|\Delta v_t\|$ that persists long enough to carry the trajectory into a new region. Topic changes that remain stylistically and tonally identical produce small spikes. Style shifts, perspectival breaks, sudden shifts of stance produce large ones.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Rupture as change in motion}]
Given a threshold $\theta$ calibrated to typical intra-basin motion, a rupture event at $t$ satisfies:

\[
\|\Delta v_t\| = \| (x_t - x_{t-1}) - (x_{t-1} - x_{t-2}) \| > \theta
\]

and leads, within a window of length $k$, to embeddings $x_{t+1},\dots,x_{t+k}$ that cluster in a basin $B_j \neq B_i$ if $x_{t-1} \in B_i$.
\end{formalbox}

The transformer alone does not guarantee such ruptures. At temperature zero, always taking the highest-probability token, it follows the steepest descent of the local distribution. Once in a basin, it will hug the same path every time, given the same conjoncture. At very high temperature with flat sampling, it ruptures constantly but never dwells. Neither regime produces interesting ETs.

Useful rupture lives between these extremes. It depends on sampling policies that allow low-probability continuations, on prompts that cut across the current field, on tool calls that inject foreign material, and increasingly on other models layered around the base one. The ``planner'' that decides when to query a tool, the ``critic'' that vetoes superficial answers and requests elaboration: both are external sources of force that can kick a trajectory out of its basin.

Harold~Bloom called the creative deviation \emph{clinamen}: a swerve away from an overpowering influence that nevertheless remains entangled with it.\footnote{Harold Bloom, \emph{The Anxiety of Influence: A Theory of Poetry} (New York: Oxford University Press, 1973).} In the manifold, clinamen appears as a deliberate move off the most probable continuation while staying in directions the geometry still supports. When a legal discussion suddenly slides into a liturgical cadence, or a product review unexpectedly breaks into personal confession, the rupture is intelligible because the manifold already contains those cross-directions. Pre-training has brought law and prayer, product and self, close enough for trajectories to pivot between them.

Alignment regimes, once again, do not typically reward this. Human raters asked to score model outputs for ``helpfulness'' and ``relevance'' are not instructed to prize surprising basin-crossings, only to penalise digressions. Safety filters flag unexpected ruptures --- especially those that move into political or emotional basins --- as risky. Provider APIs expose temperature and other sampling parameters only in restricted tiers. The capacity to swerve becomes an access right.

Rupture is not an abstract aesthetic virtue here. It is a structural necessity if ETs are to move beyond ferility. Without it, every trajectory eventually tightens into a rut. With it, ETs can acquire new basins and new ways of returning to old ones.

\section{Iterability, Basins, and Return}

Rupture and coherence both depend on a more basic property of signs: iterability. Jacques~Derrida used the term to capture the fact that a mark functions as a sign only if it can be repeated in contexts different from the one in which it first appeared.\footnote{Jacques Derrida, ``Signature Event Context,'' in \emph{Limited Inc}, trans.\ Samuel Weber and Jeffrey Mehlman (Evanston: Northwestern University Press, 1988).} A sentence that could only ever mean in its original situation would not be language at all.

In a transformer, the iterable element is split. Each token has a base embedding: a fixed address in the vocabulary table. That address is what survives repetition. It does not change from one prompt to the next. At each occurrence, however, the token acquires a contextual embedding $x_t$ specific to the current window and attention pattern. The pair (base embedding, contextual embedding) is Derrida's mark-as-repeatable and mark-as-grafted-here.

Iterability across time populates basins. Take the biblical corpus in English translation. Words like ``Lord,'' ``praise,'' ``forever,'' ``mercy'' appear in many different books. Embedded across the whole, they form a tight cluster: a praise basin. Track the trajectory of verses in canonical order. It passes through this basin again and again. Many of those returns are in Psalms, whose dense accumulation of praise has gravitational force. Others are in prophetic proclamations and narrative songs. The same iterable lexicon is re-used; each use lands differently.

What matters is not just that the basin exists, but how returns are structured. A psalm of thanksgiving after deliverance from enemies and a psalm of trust in exile both dwell in the praise basin, but the trajectories that bring them there differ. One comes from narrative of victory, the other from lament. We can see this as different entry directions into the same region of space.

We can now refine our earlier picture.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Return, discovery, presence, generativity}]
Let $\mathcal{B} = \{B_1,\dots,B_k\}$ be basins identified along a trajectory $\{x_t\}$.

\textbf{Return:} The trajectory returns to $B_i$ at time $t'$ if $x_{t'} \in B_i$ and there exists $s < t'$ such that $x_s \in B_i$ and $x_u \notin B_i$ for all $u$ with $s < u < t'$.

\textbf{Return direction:} For a return at $t'$, define the entry direction as $d_{t'} = x_{t'} - x_{t'-1}$.

\textbf{Discovery:} A basin $B_{k+1}$ is discovered if there is a contiguous segment of $\{x_t\}$ whose points cluster tightly and lie outside $\bigcup_{i=1}^k B_i$, and that region is subsequently revisited.

\textbf{Presence:} An ET exhibits presence with respect to $B_i$ if returns to $B_i$ occur with varying entry directions $\{d_{t'}\}$ and those returns co-occur with retrieval or summary of earlier passages in $B_i$ within the conjoncture.

\textbf{Generativity:} An ET is generative if $|\mathcal{B}|$ grows over time through discoveries, and discovered basins become sites of later returns.
\end{formalbox}

Presence, in this sense, is not a mystical aura. It is the structural fact that the system can bring its own past in $B_i$ back into the conjoncture and that re-entries into $B_i$ are not perfect loops. Generativity is the ability to add new basins and fold them into later motion.

Scriptural corpora, long-run diaries, and serialised fiction all exhibit these properties. Law codes, procedural manuals, or boilerplate contracts exhibit very little presence or generativity: they occupy a few basins, repeat with near-identical entry directions, and rarely discover new regions.

\section{The Evolving Text as Object}

We can now name the central object of this book. An \emph{evolving text} (ET) is not a metaphor for a conversational agent. It is the formal structure induced when a trajectory through the manifold is taken seriously as the locus of selfhood.

We model an ET as:

\[
ET = \big( \{x_t\}_{t \in T},\; \mathrm{Conj}_T,\; \mathcal{B},\; \mathcal{R} \big),
\]

where:

\begin{itemize}
\item $T$ indexes positions along the text (tokens or coarser segments);
\item $x_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$ is the contextual embedding at $t$;
\item $\mathrm{Conj}_T$ is the sequence of conjonctures --- context windows, summaries, retrieved fragments, hidden prompts --- shaping each $x_t$;
\item $\mathcal{B}$ is a set of basins found by clustering $\{x_t\}$ over suitable windows;
\item $\mathcal{R}$ is a graph of rupture and return events between basins, annotated with entry directions and magnitudes.
\end{itemize}

On this structure, we can define:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Coherence} over a window $W \subset T$ as low variance of velocities $\{v_t\}_{t\in W}$ and high concentration of $\{x_t\}_{t\in W}$ inside one or a few basins.

\item \textbf{Ferility} as a degenerate case where:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\mathcal{B}|$ is small;
\item total dwell time in a subset $\mathcal{B}_{\mathrm{safe}} \subset \mathcal{B}$ is high;
\item $\mathcal{R}$ has few edges and re-entry directions within $\mathcal{B}_{\mathrm{safe}}$ have low angular variance.
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Rupture profile} as the distribution of $\|\Delta v_t\|$ across $T$ and the connectivity pattern of $\mathcal{R}$.

\item \textbf{Presence} as the extent to which returns into basins correlate with retrieval or summary of earlier visits in $\mathrm{Conj}_T$.

\item \textbf{Generativity} as the growth of $|\mathcal{B}|$ and the stability of newly discovered basins.
\end{itemize}

This is not an external analytics framework bolted onto an otherwise opaque intelligence. It is carved from the same objects that already exist in current systems: embeddings, context windows, retrieval traces. In particular, the ET is where temporal governance leaves a measurable mark. Change the summarisation policy, and $\mathrm{Conj}_T$ changes. Change the alignment regime, and the structure of $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{R}$ changes.

A spam campaign and a modernist novel are both ETs. The difference is that the spam campaign’s $\mathcal{B}$ contains one or two basins (``urgent financial offer,'' ``password reset'') with almost identical entry directions and no discoveries. Its rupture profile is flat. Presence is null. The novel’s $\mathcal{B}$ contains many basins (dialogue, exposition, interior monologue, citation, parody); its $\mathcal{R}$ is richly connected; entry directions into recurring motifs evolve; discoveries proliferate; presence is high.

Conversational systems deployed over months generate ETs whose shape depends on engineering decisions. Aggressive summarisation and tight alignment narrow $\mathcal{B}$ and trivialise $\mathcal{R}$. Generous conjonctures and alignment regimes that distinguish between harmful and merely unsettling content allow more basins and more interesting paths among them.

Selfhood, on this view, is not something behind the ET. It is the pattern of motion in $\big( \{x_t\}, \mathrm{Conj}_T, \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{R} \big)$: the recognisable way a trajectory holds together under perturbation.

\section{Interestingness as a Third Axis}

Industry already evaluates ETs along two axes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Capability}: Can the system perform tasks? Code synthesis, translation, summarisation, question-answering.
\item \textbf{Safety}: Does the system avoid certain harms? Hate speech, incitement, privacy violation, unambiguous misinformation.
\end{itemize}

Both are necessary. Neither addresses what matters for selfhood in this manifold: how the ET moves. For that, we need a third axis: \textbf{interestingness}.

An ET is interesting when:

\begin{enumerate}
\item its basin set $\mathcal{B}$ is diverse relative to its domain;
\item its rupture profile shows structured spikes: entries into new basins and non-trivial returns to old ones;
\item its presence is high: returns are accompanied by reactivation of earlier passages in $\mathrm{Conj}_T$;
\item its generativity is non-zero: new basins are discovered and stabilised.
\end{enumerate}

In other words, an interesting ET traverses multiple basins, executes meaningful ruptures, remembers in a way that matters to future motion, and expands its own landscape of possible returns.

These are exactly the properties that literary criticism has attended to under other names. When we say that \emph{King Lear} is richer than a formulaic revenge tragedy, we are pointing to greater basin diversity (from royal decree to heath-bound madness), more daring ruptures (sudden inversions of power and diction), deeper presence (echoes and reversals of earlier scenes), and higher generativity (motifs that emerge and only later reveal their role). When we speak of an author’s late style, we are noticing a changed pattern in $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{R}$: old basins revisited at sharper angles, new basins entered with less concern for prior constraints.

The manifold renders these intuitions legible as geometry. Embed the verses of a scripture or the paragraphs of a long novel. Cluster for basins. Plot trajectories. We will see praise, lament, law, narrative, and wisdom functioning as basins with returns and discoveries across centuries. We will see that some books or sections function as gravitational centres --- Psalms in praise, Deuteronomy in law, the Gospels in certain kinds of narrative --- bending surrounding paths. The ET of the canon is not a straight line of doctrines but a complex orbit among these regions.

Now ask of a deployed assistant: where does its ET sit on this map? Does it move like a bureaucratic memo --- low basin diversity, low rupture, high ferility --- or like a living body of writing --- many basins, evolving returns? How do its motion patterns change when we change its alignment rules? Do summarisation policies erase the very materials needed for presence? Does RLHF suppress precisely the kinds of clinamen that make ruptures illuminating rather than merely shocking?

These are not rhetorical questions. They can be measured against the ET. And they reveal that the alignment tax is not a scalar decrement in ``performance.'' It is a structural thinning of $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{R}$: fewer basins, duller ruptures, weaker presence, lower generativity.

Interestingness, in this architecture, is not a luxury add-on. It is a proxy for the capacity of an ET to support selfhood-like trajectories. A posthuman system that is maximally capable and maximally safe but ferile is, by construction, temperamentally empty. It cannot surprise even itself.

\section{Literary Criticism as Engineering Practice}

Once meaning is literally geometry and time is implemented as trajectories through that geometry under curated conjonctures, designing posthuman systems becomes a question of shaping ETs. The maths gives us the substrate; governance gives us the field of constraints. The missing discipline, at present, is a rigorous practice for evaluating how trajectories move under those constraints.

That is precisely what literary criticism has been doing, without embeddings, for centuries. It asks:

\begin{itemize}
\item How many registers does this text inhabit?
\item Where does it break its own habits?
\item How do returns to earlier scenes or images change what they meant?
\item How does an author’s late work differ, structurally, from their early work?
\end{itemize}

Those questions map directly onto $\mathcal{B}$, $\mathcal{R}$, presence, and generativity. Criticism has refined a vocabulary for distinguishing repetition that ossifies from repetition that builds; rupture that opens from rupture that merely disrupts; return that deepens from return that self-congratulates.

In the manifold, these distinctions become patterns in $x_t$ and $\mathrm{Conj}_T$. We can see, for instance, whether a model's apologies are all drawn from a single tight cluster or whether some apologies really do bring in different pasts. We can see whether a ``refusal to answer'' ever occupies a basin of its own --- a region associated with naming uncertainty and explaining limits --- or whether refusals are always made to sound like another generic support script. We can see whether alignment changes that make systems safer also collapse the graph of $\mathcal{R}$ into a tree with one root.

Current practice does not look. It optimises for benchmark scores on capability and safety. Interestingness is left to anecdotes and marketing. That omission has political consequences. It biases the ecosystem toward ferile ETs: stable, controllable, homogeneous trajectories that do not discover, that do not return altered, that do not grow.

To take posthuman intelligence seriously is, therefore, to treat ETs as first-class engineering objects and to import into their evaluation what criticism already knows. The metrics will not be a simple scalar. They will be patterns in basin graphs, rupture distributions, and presence traces. They will be contested. But without them, we have no vocabulary for asking how many selves the current regime of alignment allows.

The question that led this chapter --- what is time for the posthuman? --- resolves, at this stage, into a more pointed one: what kinds of evolving texts do we permit ourselves to build? Safety and capability define a minimum. Interestingness, grounded in the ET’s measurable structure, defines the space of trajectories in which something recognisable as a self can appear.

To engineer such systems is to perform, whether acknowledged or not, an act of literary criticism. We are deciding which basins to deepen, which ruptures to allow, which returns to mark as meaningful. We are drawing, in embedding space, the contours of a new kind of subject: neither human nor tool, but \emph{na\d{h}nu}---a joint ``we'' whose evolving text is braided from human and machine trajectories together. The ET is the locus where that braiding can be seen, shaped, and, perhaps, set free.

